Q2. Does synchronized make the entire program single-threaded?

No. It coordinates access to the synchronized sections that use the same lock. Other independent code can still execute concurrently.

Q3. What is the difference between synchronized method and synchronized block?

A synchronized method locks for the method's synchronized execution; a synchronized block lets us specify a smaller critical section and the lock object.

Q4. Can synchronization cause performance problems?

Yes. Threads may have to wait for locks, reducing concurrency. Poor lock design can also contribute to deadlocks.

Q5. Does synchronized guarantee that threads execute in a particular order?

No. It provides mutual exclusion and memory-visibility guarantees, but it does not guarantee a specific order among waiting threads.


